From: NXForge layer <layer@nxforge.invalid>
Date: Thu, 1 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: upstream-pr-2532: Ocarina of Time takes the launcher's Render scale
 up to its output's own size: 1.333 draws the 3D views at 2560x1440 instead of
 1920x1080 docked

From seedds/nxnative#2532 (open; a442ddec 3e76fe65), taken early for Ocarina of Time. Drop this patch when nxnative lands #2532.

Ocarina of Time takes the launcher's Render scale up to its output's own size: 1.333 draws the 3D views at 2560x1440 instead of 1920x1080 docked

With Dynamic resolution off, the dynamic-resolution stub's level 0 now also writes level 0's scale (the first entry of the game's level list) as the game's own scale times DM_RENDER_SCALE (state +0x10), at most 1.0 of the output. The game's own scale is kept in the state page (+0x2a0) on the first call, so 1.0, the default, writes it back and nothing changes. The stub declares RENDER_SCALE 2, so the launcher offers the setting; above 1.333 the 3D views stay at the output's size.

The game reads the scale when it sets its views' sizes, at start and when the level changes, so the setting takes effect at start, as the launcher sets it.

Checked on an M5 Max (40-core GPU), docked, a window, kokiri-deck from the deck save: pass dumps in play show the main passes at 1920x1080 with 1.0 and 2560x1440 with 1.333; three alternating runs of each gave 56.2 fps / GPU p50 13.46 ms with 1.0 and 57.5 fps / 13.34 ms with 1.333. docs_patches.py --check, test_docs, test_patches, test_settings and test_launcher_settings pass.

The game note's new Render scale section also records why 3840x2160 is not done: the frame buffer can be raised to it, but the game's upscaler library has no variant above 2560x1440 and the window's textures stay that size.

Ocarina of Time outputs 3840x2160 above render scale 1.333, and 2.0 draws its 3D views native at it

Above 1.333 the output is the game's own 3840x2160 docked instead of 2560x1440, and the 3D views are drawn at 1080 x the render scale lines of it: 2.0 at 3840x2160, 1.5 at 2880x1620 with the game's upscaler filling the rest. 1.333 or less keeps every size as before.

Four sites, each found by its function's fingerprint (output_4k in patches.py):
- the output size x 1.5 at the game's init (main+0x1087b70, v131072) and on a mode change (main+0x6502e0)
- the window textures' size x 1.5 at the graphics init (main+0x346be50); without it every present crops a 2560x1440 window texture
- the render-target memory x 2.25: the app object's budget (+0x5b8, 305 MB) and block size (+0x5c0, 64 MB), which main+0x36ae9d0 copies into the allocator; without it the per-frame render targets of a 3840x2160 view do not fit, the allocator hands out a null block and the game crashes at the first frame of play (main+0x404fc)
The level-0 stub writes 1.5 x 0.75 x the render scale above 1.333, since the dynamic resolution's update and the view-size update each scale the level by 1440 / the output's height.

Checked on an M5 Max (docked, a window, kokiri-deck from the deck save, two runs each): main passes at 1920x1080 / 2560x1440 / 2880x1620 / 3840x2160 with 1.0 / 1.333 / 1.5 / 2.0 (pass dumps in play), no red line; GPU p50 13.41-13.85 ms with 1.0, 13.88-13.90 with 1.333, 14.54-14.71 with 2.0, 59-60 fps. docs_patches.py --check, test_docs, test_patches, test_settings, test_launcher_settings. Not run: handheld, a slower Mac, the iPhone.
