+## Render scale
+
+- Current: the launcher's **Render scale** (`DM_RENDER_SCALE`, 1.0 by default, up to 2.0) sets the 3D views at 1080 x the scale lines, docked. At 1.0 the game draws them at 1920x1080, level 0 of its dynamic resolution (0.75 of its 2560x1440 output), and its upscaler fills the output. 1.333 draws them at 2560x1440, the output's own size. Above 1.333 the output is the game's own 3840x2160 instead (window textures, frame buffer and the 3D views' base), and 2.0 draws the 3D views at 3840x2160, native; 1.5 draws them at 2880x1620 and the game's upscaler fills the rest.
+- How, up to 1.333: with Dynamic resolution off, the dynamic-resolution stub's level 0 also writes level 0's scale (the first entry of the game's level list) as the game's own scale times the render scale, at most 1.0. It keeps the game's own scale in the state page (`+0x2a0`) on its first call, so 1.0 writes the game's value back.
+- How, above 1.333 (`output_4k` in [`patches.py`](patches.py), every site found by its function's fingerprint): the output size x 1.5 at the init (main+0x1087b70, v131072) and on a mode change (main+0x6502e0), the window textures' size x 1.5 at the graphics init (main+0x346be50), and the render-target memory x 2.25 (the app object's budget at `+0x5b8`, 305 MB, and block size at `+0x5c0`, 64 MB, copied into the allocator by main+0x36ae9d0). Level 0 is then 1.5 x 0.75 x the render scale: the dynamic resolution's update (main+0x805aa0) and the view-size update (main+0x31d3200) each scale it by 1440 / the output's height. Found with probes of the view-size update in play, the host's write watch (`DM_PROBE_WATCH`) on the view's size fields (written by main+0xe82f30, which holds the base to a render buffer's size) and a Ghidra decompile of the allocator (main+0x3ed00).
+- What each one fixes: without the window's size the frame buffer is 3840x2160 and every present crops a 2560x1440 window texture; without level 0 above 1.0 the 3D views stay at 2560x1440 (or 1707x960 at a level of 1.0); without the memory the per-frame render targets of a 3840x2160 view do not fit the budget, the allocator hands out a null block, and the game crashes at the first frame of play (a read at main+0x404fc).
+- The game reads the scale when it sets its views' sizes, at start and when the level changes, not every frame. The setting takes effect at start, as the launcher sets it; written into the running game (`DM_CTL`) it has no effect until the level changes. The 3840x2160 output is set at start too.
+- Measured on an M5 Max (40-core GPU, 64 GB, 2026-11-12, docked, a window, [`bench.tsv`](bench.tsv) `kokiri-deck` from the deck save, two runs of each): the main passes at 1920x1080 with 1.0, 2560x1440 with 1.333, 2880x1620 with 1.5 and 3840x2160 with 2.0 (pass dumps in play). 1.0: 56.7 and 60.0 fps, GPU p50 13.41 and 13.85 ms; 1.333: 60.0 fps both, 13.90 and 13.88 ms; 2.0: 60.0 and 59.0 fps, 14.54 and 14.71 ms. So native 3840x2160 costs about 0.7 ms of GPU time a frame over 2560x1440 here, and the game holds 60 fps; the frame's cost on Apple GPUs hardly depends on its resolution ([Frame rate](#frame-rate)). Not measured: a slower Mac, handheld (the output stubs scale the handheld size too; not run), the iPhone.
 
